"""
The KVLCC2 research tanker at full scale, as a single-screw ship with an ideal speed
governor: the counterpart of `StandardShip` for a published hull.

What is taken from the literature (Yasukawa and Yoshimura, *Introduction of MMG standard
method for ship maneuvering predictions*, J. Mar. Sci. Technol. 20 (2015) 37–52, open
access; table and page of every value are in
`assets/presets/Hull/kvlcc2_full_scale.provenance.toml`):

- `hydro`: `HydrodynamicXYY` with the file `presets/Hull/kvlcc2_full_scale.toml`:
  principal particulars, resistance coefficient, added masses and all sixteen hull
  derivatives of the source's Table 3.
- `ship`: displacement 312 600 m³ (320 415 t at 1025 kg/m³), centre of gravity 11.2 m
  forward of midship, yaw gyradius 0.25 Lpp (Table 1 and the simulation conditions).
- `prop`: diameter 9.86 m, open-water thrust `Kt = 0.2931 - 0.2753 J - 0.1385 J²`,
  thrust deduction 0.220, full-scale wake fraction 0.35. The open-water torque
  `10 Kq = 0.3071 - 0.1856 J - 0.2045 J²` is a quadratic fit of the open-water test of the
  same 0.0896 m model propeller published with the SIMMAN 2008 workshop data; the same
  fit of its thrust column returns the source's thrust polynomial to four digits. That
  file warns that the torque of so small a propeller is a guide only.
- `rudder`: area 112.5 m² and height 15.80 m (chord 7.12 m), `a_H` = 0.312,
  flow-straightening coefficient 0.395 in a port turn and 0.640 in a starboard turn
  (the source's values for `β_R < 0` and `β_R > 0` in its starboard-positive axes; `Rudder`
  uses `Gamma_R_Neg` in a port turn, so 0.395 is `Gamma_R_Neg`), steering rate 1.76 °/s.
- approach speed 15.5 kn; the shaft speed that holds it, 106.7 rpm, follows from the
  resistance coefficient, thrust deduction, wake and thrust polynomial above.

What is **not** from the source, because its model has three degrees of freedom and
this one six. These are assumptions made so the ship floats upright; they do not enter
the surge, sway and yaw forces:

- hydrostatics as a wall-sided prism: displacement proportional to draft, centre of
  buoyancy at 0.52 of the draft and at the longitudinal centre of gravity, metacentric
  radii `259.6 / T` and `7901 / T` m;
- centre of gravity 18 m above the keel, roll and pitch gyradii at the `ShipBody`
  defaults, heave, roll and pitch damping of `HydrodynamicZRP` with `Cw` = 0.9;
- propeller 6 m forward of the aft perpendicular and 5.5 m above the keel, rudder stock
  at the aft perpendicular, propeller-to-rudder distance 7.4 m, shaft inertia.

What the existing components cannot represent of the source's model: the MMG rudder
force model and its coefficients (`t_R`, `x'_H`, `ε`, `κ`, `l'_R`, `f_α`), since `Rudder`
uses NACA 0015 lift and drag tables with its own slipstream model; the change of the
propeller wake with drift; resistance on the total speed rather than the surge speed.

## Resistance: two values, for two purposes

`R2_resistance` is the coefficient of the quadratic resistance law of `hydro`.

- Default 75 046 N s²/m²: the source's `R'0` = 0.022. That is the resistance coefficient
  of the 2.9 m captive model. It is right for reproducing the model-scale balance of
  the manoeuvring tests; at full scale it asks for 79 MW at 15.5 kn and 106.7 rev/min,
  which is not a ship's powering point. `KVLCC2Ship` keeps it so its results are
  unchanged.
- 26 909 N s²/m² (`PoweredKVLCC2Ship`): a full-scale estimate. The source says its
  full-scale runs used a resistance from "a 3-dimensional extrapolation method based on
  Schoenherr's frictional resistance coefficient formula" but prints neither that
  resistance nor the form factor. This value repeats the method with the wetted
  surface 27 194 m² from the SIMMAN 2008 data and the wave-making part taken as zero:
  the model's total coefficient 5.385e-3 is scaled by the ratio of the Schoenherr
  friction coefficients at the two Reynolds numbers (1.396e-3 / 3.893e-3), giving
  1.931e-3 on the wetted surface, 1.71 MN and 13.6 MW effective power at 15.5 kn. No
  roughness allowance. With the form factor unknown this is the low end; the two-
  dimensional (Froude) extrapolation of the same numbers gives 40 241 N s²/m², 2.56 MN.
  It is an estimate by a stated method, not a published powering prediction; none was
  found for this hull.

Extending components set `rudder_order` [deg, positive to port] and drive the shaft at
`shaft.spline_a` (positive rotation is ahead).
"""
partial component KVLCC2Hull
  "Initial speed along world x: 15.5 kn"
  parameter U0::Velocity = 7.973889
  "Shaft inertia including the propeller and entrained water (assumed)"
  parameter J_shaft::Real = 4e5
  "Coefficient of the quadratic resistance law [N s²/m²]; see the two values in the description"
  parameter R2_resistance::Real = 75046.4
  world = MultibodyComponents.World(n = [0, 0, -1], g = 9.80665, render = false, nominal_length = 320)
  ship = ShipBody(Lpp = 320, B = 58, Cb = 0.810, mass = 320415000, CoG = [171.2, 0, 18], I_zz = 320415000 * 6400, n_Disp = 1, Disp_Table = [[15404567.3, 1]], n_LCB = 1, LCB_Table = [[171.2, 0]], n_VCB = 1, VCB_Table = [[0.52, 1]], BMt_Table = [[259.568, -1]], BMl_Table = [[7901.23, -1]], ini_Pos = [0, 0, -20.8], ini_Vel = [U0, 0, 0])
  hydro = HydrodynamicXYY(apply "dyad://DyadShip/presets/Hull/kvlcc2_full_scale.toml", R2 = R2_resistance)
  zrp = HydrodynamicZRP(Lpp = 320, B = 58, mass = 320415000, Cw = 0.9, GM = 5.3, GM_L = 372)
  prop_mount = MultibodyComponents.FixedTranslation(r = [6, 0, 5.5], render = false)
  prop = Propeller1Q(Diameter = 9.86, Lpp = 320, B = 58, Cb = 0.810, WakeFraction = 0.35, ThrustDeduction = 0.220, Rudder_distance = 7.4, open_water_polynomial = 1, Kt0 = 0.2931, Kt1 = -0.2753, Kt2 = -0.1385, Kq0 = 0.030710, Kq1 = -0.018559, Kq2 = -0.020451)
  shaft = RotationalComponents.Components.Inertia(J = J_shaft)
  rudder_mount = MultibodyComponents.FixedTranslation(r = [0, 0, 16.5], render = false)
  rudder = Rudder(Lpp = 320, B = 58, Cb = 0.810, T = 20.8, C = 7.12, s = 15.8, Rudder_distance = 7.4, a_h = 0.312, Gamma_R_Pos = 0.640, Gamma_R_Neg = 0.395, MaxRudderAngularSpeed = 1.76)
  "Rudder angle order [deg], positive to port"
  variable rudder_order::Real
relations
  connect(ship.frame_a, hydro.frame_a, zrp.frame_a, prop_mount.frame_a, rudder_mount.frame_a)
  connect(prop_mount.frame_b, prop.frame_a)
  connect(rudder_mount.frame_b, rudder.frame_a)
  connect(ship.Draft, hydro.MeanDraft)
  connect(ship.Draft, zrp.MeanDraft)
  connect(ship.Displacement, hydro.Displacement)
  connect(ship.Displacement, zrp.Displacement)
  hydro.Current_x = 0
  hydro.Current_y = 0
  prop.Current_x = 0
  prop.Current_y = 0
  rudder.Current_x = 0
  rudder.Current_y = 0
  connect(prop.Propeller_speed, rudder.Propeller_speed)
  connect(prop.Propeller_flow_diameter, rudder.Propeller_flow_diameter)
  connect(prop.Wake_Fraction, rudder.Wake_Fraction)
  connect(shaft.spline_b, prop.flange)
  rudder.Rudder_Order = rudder_order
end

"""
`KVLCC2Hull` with its shaft driven by an ideal speed governor that tracks `shaft_rpm`:
the counterpart of `StandardShip`, used for the manoeuvring comparison. No engine.
"""
partial component KVLCC2Ship
  extends KVLCC2Hull
  governor = RotationalComponents.Sources.VelocitySource()
  ground = RotationalComponents.Components.Fixed()
  "Shaft speed order [rpm]"
  variable shaft_rpm::Real
relations
  initial shaft.phi = 0
  connect(governor.support, ground.spline)
  connect(governor.spline, shaft.spline_a)
  governor.w_ref = shaft_rpm * π / 30
end

"""
`KVLCC2Hull` prepared for a power plant, with the full-scale resistance estimate: the
counterpart of `PoweredSingleScrewShip`. Extending components add one subcomponent
`plant` that extends `ShipPowerPlantPorts`, connect `plant.propeller_flange` to
`shaft.spline_a`, and set `plant.shaft_speed_order` [rpm], `plant.hotel_power_demand` [W]
and `rudder_order` [deg]. `service_distance` [m] is the integral of the forward speed.

At the propeller the plant sees the torque of the published open-water curve: about
2.7 MN·m and 21 MW at 15.5 kn and 74 rev/min with this resistance estimate.
"""
partial component PoweredKVLCC2Ship
  extends KVLCC2Hull(R2_resistance = 26908.9)
  "Distance travelled [m]"
  variable service_distance::Length
relations
  initial service_distance = 0
  der(service_distance) = ship.Surge
end

"""
The powered KVLCC2 as a component that another model can hold as a subcomponent (a
partial component cannot be one): `PoweredKVLCC2Ship` with its propeller shaft and
rudder order brought out as connectors. Connect a plant to `propeller_flange` and give
`rudder_order_deg` [deg, positive to port]. It contains the `world` of its multibody
parts, so the model that holds it must not add another.
"""
component KVLCC2Vessel
  extends PoweredKVLCC2Ship
  "Propeller shaft; positive rotation is ahead"
  propeller_flange = Spline()
  "Rudder angle order [deg], positive to port"
  rudder_order_deg = RealInput()
  "Forward speed [m/s]"
  speed = RealOutput()
relations
  connect(propeller_flange, shaft.spline_a)
  rudder_order = rudder_order_deg
  speed = ship.Surge
end

"""
`KVLCC2Vessel` held as a subcomponent, with a plant beside it: the powering run of
`KVLCC2Powering` built from two components instead of by extension.
"""
test component KVLCC2VesselWithPlant
  parameter rpm::Real = 73.7
  vessel = KVLCC2Vessel()
  plant = IdealGovernorPlant()
relations
  connect(plant.propeller_flange, vessel.propeller_flange)
  plant.shaft_speed_order = rpm
  plant.hotel_power_demand = 0
  vessel.rudder_order_deg = -(3 * vessel.ship.Yaw + 80 * vessel.ship.YawRate) * 180 / π
end

analysis KVLCC2VesselWithPlantTransient
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 600.0)
  model = KVLCC2VesselWithPlant()
end

"""
Straight-line powering run of the KVLCC2 with a plant in the plant slot: the shaft order
is held at `rpm` and the ship runs at the speed where thrust balances resistance. The
plant is `IdealGovernorPlant`, which has no engine; the library's diesel engine is some
twenty times too small for this ship.

The rudder keeps the course with a proportional-derivative law on heading and yaw rate.
Without it the ship does not hold a straight course: with the published derivatives the
hull is directionally unstable, and the reaction to the propeller torque is enough to
start a slow turn that takes the speed down within the hour.
"""
test component KVLCC2Powering
  extends PoweredKVLCC2Ship
  parameter rpm::Real = 73.7
  "Course-keeping gains: rudder degrees per degree of heading error, and per degree per second of yaw rate"
  parameter Kp::Real = 3
  parameter Kd::Real = 80
  plant = IdealGovernorPlant()
relations
  connect(plant.propeller_flange, shaft.spline_a)
  plant.shaft_speed_order = rpm
  plant.hotel_power_demand = 0
  rudder_order = -(Kp * ship.Yaw + Kd * ship.YawRate) * 180 / π
end

analysis KVLCC2PoweringTransient
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 1500.0)
  model = KVLCC2Powering()
end

"""
Turning circle of the full-scale KVLCC2 from 15.5 kn at a constant 106.7 rpm: the rudder
goes to `rudder_deg` (positive to port) at `t_rudder`. Advance and tactical diameter are
read from the track (`ship.pos_x`, `ship.pos_y`) at 90° and 180° change of heading and
compared with the published values by `scripts/validate_kvlcc2.jl`.
"""
test component KVLCC2TurningCircle
  extends KVLCC2Ship
  parameter rpm::Real = 106.7106
  parameter rudder_deg::Real = 35
  parameter t_rudder::Time = 100
relations
  rudder_order = ifelse(time > t_rudder, rudder_deg, 0)
  shaft_rpm = rpm
end

analysis KVLCC2TurningCircleTransient
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 2400.0, tstops = [100.0])
  model = KVLCC2TurningCircle()
end
